window.BANK={"id": "m2", "title": "Mock Exam #2", "minutes": 100, "mode": "practice", "v": 1, "cases": {"northwind": {"title": "Northwind Traders: multilingual support agent", "short": "Northwind support agent", "tabs": [{"name": "Overview", "items": ["Northwind Traders is a home-goods retailer with 140 stores across Canada and the United States and a fast-growing online shop. About a third of its customers in Quebec prefer to be served in French, and a growing share of customers in the United States prefer Spanish.", "The customer service team handles about 9,000 chats and 3,500 phone calls a day. Volumes roughly double during the November and December holiday season and again during the January returns season, when most contacts are about refunds, exchanges and late deliveries.", "Northwind plans to replace its scripted chatbot and its touch-tone phone menu with **Ask Northwind**: a support agent built in Microsoft Foundry that answers order, delivery and returns questions in English, French and Spanish, both in web chat and on the phone.", "The brand team wants Ask Northwind to sound like one consistent Northwind voice in every language and on every channel. Northwind's customers include many teenagers, who often shop with a parent's account."]}, {"name": "Existing environment", "items": ["**Foundry:** One Foundry resource in Canada East with one project named **support**. The platform team created the resource and the project by using the Azure CLI. The project has a gpt-5-mini deployment. An Application Insights resource is already connected to the project.", "**Policies:** Returns, warranty and delivery policies, about 600 pages in English only, are indexed in an Azure AI Search index named **policies**. The index uses hybrid search with a semantic configuration, and agents in the project already query it successfully.", "**Orders API:** Order data is exposed by the **Orders API** in Azure API Management. It publishes an OpenAPI 3.0 description and accepts only Microsoft Entra ID tokens; API keys were disabled last year. Its operations are `getOrder`, `getDeliveryStatus` and `createReturn`.", "**Phone:** A cloud contact-centre platform streams call audio to a small Northwind phone service that runs in Azure Container Apps. The container app has a user-assigned managed identity. Calls are recorded and kept in Blob Storage for 90 days.", "**People:** The customer service operations team owns the agent's instructions and tests changes in the Foundry portal. A team lead, Ana, will publish the finished agent to Microsoft Teams so that store staff can look up orders for customers at the counter. A capacity-planning team reviews model quota before every holiday season."]}, {"name": "Planned design", "items": ["Ask Northwind will be a prompt agent with two tools: the Azure AI Search tool over the **policies** index, and a tool for the Orders API.", "The web chat widget and the phone service will both call the same agent. The phone service turns the caller's speech into text, sends the text to the agent, and reads the agent's reply back to the caller with a neural text to speech voice by using SSML.", "Policy content will stay in English only. The legal team reviews every policy change and doesn't want to review or approve copies in other languages.", "Replies on the phone often include order numbers, such as NW4471B, and return authorisation codes, such as RA88K2.", "Each week, the operations team will review a sample of complete customer conversations from both channels to decide which instructions to change."]}, {"name": "Requirements", "items": ["**Identity:** No API keys may be used anywhere. Each service uses a managed identity with least privilege. The phone service may only call Ask Northwind.", "**Orders:** The agent must call the Orders API itself as a tool. The chat widget and the phone service must not run order lookups or returns in their own code.", "**Language:** The agent must reply in the language the customer is using. Northwind won't maintain translated copies of the policy content, and it wants as few extra services as possible in the chat path.", "**Speech:** On the phone, order numbers and return codes must be read out one character at a time, so callers can write them down the first time they hear them.", "**Safety:** Because many customers are teenagers, replies must block sexual and violent content at every severity level, including low severity.", "**Quality:** Each week, the operations team must score complete conversations, not single replies, for whether the customer seemed satisfied by the end of the conversation.", "**Access:** Ana must be able to publish the agent to Microsoft Teams. The capacity-planning team must be able to view model quota, and nothing more."]}, {"name": "Problem statements", "items": ["In a phone pilot, the agent read \"NW4471B\" aloud as \"N W four thousand four hundred seventy-one B\". Many callers asked for the number to be repeated.", "A pilot customer who wrote in French received an answer in English, apparently because every retrieved policy passage was in English.", "A developer suggested giving the phone service's identity the Foundry User role \"so it can do whatever it needs later\"."]}]}, "litware": {"title": "Litware Pumps: supplier document pipeline", "short": "Litware document pipeline", "tabs": [{"name": "Overview", "items": ["Litware Pumps manufactures industrial pumps at three plants in Germany, Poland and Mexico. Every delivery of steel, castings, seals and motors arrives with supplier documents: certificates of conformity, material test reports and packing slips.", "Today, quality engineers check every document by hand against the purchase order before parts can be used. The backlog regularly reaches six days, and production lines wait for released parts.", "Litware plans an automated pipeline that extracts fields from every supplier document, sends uncertain results to an engineer for review, and makes all documents searchable by an engineering assistant agent built in Microsoft Foundry.", "**Business goals:** release parts within one day of arrival; cut manual checking by 70%; and give engineers answers that cite the source document."]}, {"name": "Documents and media", "items": ["**Certificates of conformity:** These come from about 300 suppliers, and each supplier uses its own layout. Some are scanned paper and some are digital PDFs. New suppliers are added every quarter.", "**Material test reports:** Between 2 and 20 pages each, with tables of chemical composition and mechanical properties, followed by a heat-treatment section that often runs across several pages.", "**Packing slips:** All packing slips come from Litware's single logistics partner and use one fixed layout that hasn't changed in five years. The quality team labelled 60 sample packing slips for an earlier project, and the labels are still available.", "**Nameplate photos:** Receiving staff photograph the nameplate of every purchased motor with a tablet, about 2,000 photos a day across the plants. Nameplates show the serial number, model, voltage and power rating in small print.", "**Inspection videos:** Each plant records 30-minute videos of incoming inspections for auditors. Each video shows an inspector unpacking the delivery, measuring sample parts and recording any damage. Auditors want to jump to the part of a video where a specific delivery step happened rather than watch the whole recording.", "**Volumes:** Across the three plants, Litware receives about 1,500 supplier documents on a normal working day and up to 4,000 at the end of each quarter, when suppliers clear their order books. Documents are in German, Polish, Spanish and English."]}, {"name": "Existing environment", "items": ["**Foundry:** One Foundry resource in Germany West Central, with a project named **quality** that has gpt-5-mini and text-embedding-3-large deployments. Content Understanding default model deployments have been set for the resource.", "**Storage:** Supplier documents arrive over SFTP in a Blob Storage account named **litdocs**. Last month, the security team set public network access on **litdocs** to Disabled; the account is now reachable only through private endpoints.", "**Search:** An Azure AI Search service on the Standard tier, with role-based access control enabled. No index has been built yet.", "**ERP:** Litware's ERP system accepts approved document fields through an existing integration and marks parts as released.", "**Engineering assistant:** A prompt agent is planned in the **quality** project. Engineers will reach it through an internal web app that runs with its own managed identity."]}, {"name": "Requirements", "items": ["**Certificates:** Extract the certificate number, supplier name, material grade and batch number, with a confidence score for each field. No one will label certificates for training.", "**Test reports:** Give engineers a two-sentence summary of each report's heat-treatment section.", "**Packing slips:** Extraction must not depend on any large language model deployment.", "**Review:** Any field with confidence below 0.85 must go to a quality engineer before it reaches the ERP system. Other fields go to the ERP system directly.", "**Search:** Every document must be indexed in Azure AI Search with headings and tables kept intact, chunked and vectorized. The indexer must read from **litdocs** without public network access being turned back on.", "**Security:** No API keys may be used for Search or Storage. The engineering web app may only query the index. Apply least privilege.", "**Nameplates:** Extract the four nameplate values from each photo with a confidence score and the location on the image where each value was read, so that reviewers can check it quickly.", "**Audit:** Every extracted value that reaches the ERP system must be traceable to the document it came from, for at least seven years.", "**Cost:** Prefer the cheapest option that meets each requirement."]}, {"name": "Problem statements", "items": ["A pilot indexer run against **litdocs** failed with an authorization error after public network access was disabled.", "A prototype that sent nameplate photos to gpt-5-mini sometimes returned serial numbers with one or two wrong characters, and the shape of its answers varied from photo to photo.", "A developer suggested putting the search service's admin key in the engineering web app \"to keep things simple\"."]}]}}, "items": [{"id": "m2-01", "type": "single", "skill": "D1 · Plan and manage", "stem": "A returns agent in your Foundry project must include the policy code RP-30, exactly as written, in every answer about refunds. You need an automated pre-release check on 2,000 test answers that is deterministic, adds no model cost, and needs no judge deployment. Which evaluator should you use?", "options": ["String Checker", "Groundedness Pro", "Response Completeness", "Task Adherence"], "answer": 0, "runner": 3, "runnerWhy": "it can judge whether the agent followed its rules, but it uses an LLM judge on your deployment, so it's neither deterministic nor free.", "think": {"premise": "That every quality check needs an AI judge. When the rule is a fixed string, a deterministic check is cheaper and repeatable.", "map": [["A fixed phrase must appear", "Deterministic rule", "String Checker"], ["Did it follow its instructions?", "Judgement", "Task Adherence (LLM judge)"], ["Is it supported by passages?", "Grounding", "Groundedness Pro (Foundry-billed)"], ["Did it cover the reference answer?", "Needs ground truth", "Response Completeness"]], "apply": [["\"exactly as written\"", "A string match, not a judgement."], ["\"deterministic, adds no model cost, and needs no judge deployment\"", "Rules out every AI-assisted evaluator and Groundedness Pro, which Foundry bills."]], "rule": "Use a deterministic check, such as String Checker, wherever the rule can be written as a match.", "guide": "Apply responsible AI instrumentation, including evaluators, safety evaluations, and explanation tooling."}, "eli5": "Use a spell-checker to see if a word is there; don't hire an editor.", "learn": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/concepts/built-in-evaluators"}, {"id": "m2-02", "type": "single", "skill": "D1 · Plan and manage", "stem": "Your warranty agent must run the same four tool actions, in order, for every replacement request: verify purchase, check stock, reserve item and create shipment. You have the expected action list for 300 test cases, and the team has no budget for judge-model tokens. Which evaluator measures how closely the agent follows the expected path?", "options": ["Intent Resolution", "Task Navigation Efficiency", "Tool Call Accuracy", "Task Completion"], "answer": 1, "runner": 2, "runnerWhy": "it looks at tool calls, but it needs a judge model, which the budget rules out, and it doesn't compare the run with an expected sequence.", "think": {"premise": "That all agent evaluators need a judge. Task Navigation Efficiency compares actions with expected actions, with no judge.", "map": [["Actions vs an expected path", "Process, ground truth", "Task Navigation Efficiency (no judge)"], ["Right tool, right arguments", "Process, judged", "Tool Call Accuracy"], ["Finished the job", "Outcome, judged", "Task Completion"], ["Understood the request", "Outcome, judged", "Intent Resolution"]], "apply": [["\"the same four tool actions, in order\"", "An expected path to compare against."], ["\"no budget for judge-model tokens\"", "Only the one agent evaluator that needs no judge fits."]], "rule": "Task Navigation Efficiency: needs expected actions, needs no judge. Every other agent evaluator uses your judge's tokens.", "guide": "Apply responsible AI instrumentation, including evaluators, safety evaluations, and explanation tooling."}, "eli5": "Compare the delivery van's route with the planned route; no inspector needed.", "learn": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/concepts/built-in-evaluators"}, {"id": "m2-03", "type": "single", "skill": "D1 · Plan and manage", "stem": "A document-summary service sends a steady, round-the-clock load to a gpt-5 deployment. The business needs consistent latency, there is no data-residency requirement, and finance has approved a monthly capacity commitment. You need to maximise the capacity that the deployment can draw on. Which deployment type should you choose?", "options": ["Data Zone Provisioned", "Global Provisioned", "Global Standard", "Global Batch"], "answer": 1, "runner": 0, "runnerWhy": "it also reserves capacity with predictable latency, but it limits processing to one data zone, a restriction nobody asked for that shrinks the capacity pool.", "think": {"premise": "That Data Zone is always the safer choice. Without a residency rule, Global gives the largest pool.", "map": [["Steady load, predictable latency, commitment OK", "Reserved", "Provisioned"], ["No residency rule", "Widest pool", "Global"], ["Pay per token, variable latency", "Shared", "Standard"], ["Results within 24 hours", "Async", "Batch"]], "apply": [["\"consistent latency\" and \"capacity commitment\"", "Provisioned."], ["\"no data-residency requirement\"", "Rules out the Data Zone restriction."], ["\"round-the-clock\"", "Rules out Batch."]], "rule": "Predictable latency + commitment → Provisioned; no residency rule → Global.", "guide": "Choose appropriate deployment options."}, "eli5": "Book a reserved lane on the whole motorway network, not just the national roads.", "learn": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/foundry-models/concepts/deployment-types"}, {"id": "m2-04", "type": "single", "skill": "D1 · Plan and manage", "stem": "A marketing assistant in your Foundry project drafts product descriptions and blog posts. Legal requires that any response reproducing known third-party text, such as song lyrics or published news articles, be blocked before users see it. The deployment already blocks hate and violence. What should you add to the deployment's guardrail?", "options": ["A protected material for code control on output", "A user prompt attack control on user input", "A protected material for text control on output", "A groundedness control on output"], "answer": 2, "runner": 0, "runnerWhy": "right family, wrong content type: it detects code from public repositories, not lyrics or articles.", "think": {"premise": "That copyright risk is a grounding problem. Protected material detection is its own control, split into text and code.", "map": [["Known lyrics, articles, recipes", "Copyright, text", "Protected material for text"], ["Code copied from public repos", "Copyright, code", "Protected material for code"], ["Claims not in sources", "Grounding", "Groundedness (preview)"], ["User tries to override rules", "Attack", "User prompt attack"]], "apply": [["\"reproducing known third-party text, such as song lyrics\"", "Protected material for text."], ["\"blocked before users see it\"", "The response, so the output point."]], "rule": "Protected material: text (lyrics, articles) or code (public repos), checked on output.", "guide": "Configure safety filters, guardrails, risk detection, and content moderation."}, "eli5": "Check the essay against the library before it's handed in.", "learn": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/guardrails/guardrails-overview"}, {"id": "m2-05", "type": "single", "skill": "D1 · Plan and manage", "stem": "A model deployment's guardrail blocks violence at the Medium level on input and output. Last week a developer assigned a new guardrail, containing only a user prompt attack control, to the agent that uses this deployment. Testers now report violent replies from the agent. You need to restore violence blocking for the agent. What should you do?", "options": ["Add a violence control to the agent's guardrail", "Raise the deployment's violence level to High", "Add a violence control at the tool call point", "Add violence to the agent's forbidden topics"], "answer": 0, "runner": 1, "runnerWhy": "it tightens the model's guardrail, but the agent's own guardrail fully overrides the model's, so the agent never sees the change.", "think": {"premise": "That an agent's guardrail adds to its model's. Once an agent has its own guardrail, the model's guardrail no longer applies to it.", "map": [["Agent has no guardrail", "Inheritance", "Uses the deployment's guardrail"], ["Agent has its own guardrail", "Override", "Only the agent's controls apply"], ["Instructions", "Guidance", "Not enforcement"]], "apply": [["\"assigned a new guardrail ... to the agent\"", "Override: the model's violence control stopped applying."], ["\"violent replies\"", "Replies are output; add the control to the agent's guardrail."]], "rule": "An agent's guardrail fully overrides its model's; with none assigned, it inherits.", "guide": "Configure safety filters, guardrails, risk detection, and content moderation."}, "eli5": "A new house rulebook replaces the old one entirely; copy over the rules you still want.", "learn": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/guardrails/guardrails-overview"}, {"id": "m2-06", "type": "multi", "pick": 2, "skill": "D1 · Plan and manage", "stem": "Continuous evaluation runs Relevance, Coherence, Fluency and Task Adherence on every response of a busy production agent, and the judge model's token bill now exceeds the agent's own model bill. You must keep tracking quality trends over time. Which two actions reduce the evaluation cost? Each correct answer presents a complete solution.", "options": ["Score at the conversation level as well as the turn level", "Switch the judge to a larger reasoning model", "Move the evaluation to the agents playground", "Drop evaluators that answer no question you act on", "Lower the continuous evaluation sampling rate"], "answer": [3, 4], "runner": 2, "runnerWhy": "playground evaluations are billed too, and they only score playground chats, not production traffic.", "think": {"premise": "That quality monitoring must score every answer with every evaluator. A sample shows the trend; each evaluator is roughly one judge call per answer.", "map": [["Judge cost too high", "Volume", "Lower the sampling rate"], ["Judge cost too high", "Breadth", "Fewer, purposeful evaluators"], ["Bigger judge", "Price per call", "Costs more, not less"]], "apply": [["\"on every response\"", "Sampling cuts calls."], ["\"Relevance, Coherence, Fluency and Task Adherence\"", "Four judge calls per answer; drop the ones you don't act on."]], "rule": "AI-assisted evaluators spend your judge's tokens: sample live traffic and run only evaluators tied to a decision.", "guide": "Manage quotas, scaling, rate limits, and cost footprints for model and agent workloads."}, "eli5": "Taste one spoonful from each pot, and only for the dishes you'd actually change.", "learn": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/concepts/built-in-evaluators"}, {"id": "m2-07", "type": "yesno", "skill": "D1 · Plan and manage", "stem": "You are assigning Foundry roles for a new department that will build agents and publish some of them. For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "statements": [{"text": "Foundry Owner can create projects and also build agents in them.", "answer": true, "why": "It combines Account Owner's management with building."}, {"text": "Foundry Project Manager can assign any built-in role to members of its projects.", "answer": false, "why": "It can assign only Foundry User."}, {"text": "The renamed Foundry roles kept their original role definition IDs.", "answer": true, "why": "Scripts that use role IDs keep working."}, {"text": "Cognitive Services OpenAI User is the recommended role for calling models in a Foundry project.", "answer": false, "why": "Use Foundry roles for Foundry resources; Cognitive Services roles are for standalone resources."}], "think": {"premise": "That a manager role can hand out any role, and that the old OpenAI roles still fit Foundry. Project Manager assigns only Foundry User; Foundry resources use Foundry roles.", "map": null, "apply": null, "rule": "Foundry Owner: manage + build. Project Manager: build, publish, assign Foundry User only. Role IDs survived the rename.", "guide": "Configure security, including managed identity, private networking, keyless credentials, and role policies."}, "eli5": "The team lead can give out visitor badges, not master keys.", "learn": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/concepts/rbac-foundry"}, {"id": "m2-08", "type": "yesno", "skill": "D1 · Plan and manage", "stem": "You are choosing evaluators for a release pipeline that tests a RAG app and an agent. For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "statements": [{"text": "Groundedness Pro returns a pass or fail result rather than a 1 to 5 score.", "answer": true, "why": "It's pass/fail from Content Safety; Groundedness is 1 to 5."}, {"text": "Quality Grader can be added to a new evaluation run to score agent output.", "answer": false, "why": "It's deprecated and can no longer run; use Output Quality and Tool Use Quality."}, {"text": "The Output Quality bundle passes only if every applicable component passes.", "answer": true, "why": "Skipped components don't fail it."}, {"text": "Risk and safety evaluators require you to deploy your own judge model.", "answer": false, "why": "They run on Microsoft's side and are billed by Foundry."}], "think": {"premise": "That every evaluator needs your own judge, and that old evaluators still run. Safety evaluators are Microsoft-hosted; Quality Grader is gone.", "map": null, "apply": null, "rule": "Groundedness Pro: pass/fail, no deployment. Quality Grader: deprecated. Bundles: all applicable parts must pass. Safety: Microsoft-hosted.", "guide": "Apply responsible AI instrumentation, including evaluators, safety evaluations, and explanation tooling."}, "eli5": "Some exams are marked by the board, some by your own teacher, and one paper has been withdrawn.", "learn": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/concepts/built-in-evaluators"}, {"id": "m2-09", "type": "code", "skill": "D1 · Plan and manage", "stem": "A capacity-planning job runs as a user-assigned managed identity. Before each deployment it must read the remaining model quota in every region of the subscription, and it must not be able to deploy models, build agents or change any resource. How should you complete the command?", "code": "az role assignment create \\\n  --assignee-object-id $PLANNER_MI --assignee-principal-type ServicePrincipal \\\n  --role \"[[0]]\" \\\n  --scope [[1]]", "blanks": [{"options": ["Cognitive Services Usages Reader", "Foundry User", "Foundry Account Owner"], "answer": 0, "why": "reads quota and nothing else; Foundry User builds agents, Account Owner deploys models."}, {"options": ["/subscriptions/$SUB_ID", "/subscriptions/$SUB_ID/resourceGroups/rg-ai", "$FOUNDRY_RESOURCE_ID"], "answer": 0, "why": "quota is read at subscription scope; resource group or resource scope doesn't work."}], "think": {"premise": "That quota belongs to the Foundry resource. Quota is a subscription-level view, so the reader role must sit there.", "map": null, "apply": null, "rule": "Quota visibility: Cognitive Services Usages Reader at subscription scope.", "guide": "Manage quotas, scaling, rate limits, and cost footprints for model and agent workloads."}, "eli5": "To read the whole fuel gauge, you need a pass for the whole depot, not one pump.", "learn": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/concepts/rbac-foundry"}, {"id": "m2-10", "type": "code", "skill": "D1 · Plan and manage", "stem": "Customer product reviews must be screened with Azure AI Content Safety before they're published. A review is rejected if it matches the company's competitor-names blocklist, or if any harm category is rated at severity 4 or higher. You need to complete the Python code. How should you complete the code?", "code": "opts = AnalyzeTextOptions(text=review, [[0]]=[\"competitor-names\"],\n    halt_on_blocklist_hit=True)\nr = client.analyze_text(opts)\nblocked = bool(r.blocklists_match) or any(\n    c.[[1]] >= 4 for c in r.categories_analysis)", "blanks": [{"options": ["blocklist_names", "blocklists", "custom_terms"], "answer": 0, "why": "the AnalyzeTextOptions parameter that names the blocklists to check."}, {"options": ["severity", "score", "confidence"], "answer": 0, "why": "each category result carries a severity level, not a score or a confidence."}], "think": {"premise": "That moderation returns a probability. Content Safety returns a severity per harm category, plus any blocklist matches.", "map": null, "apply": null, "rule": "analyze_text(blocklist_names=[...]) → blocklists_match + categories_analysis[].severity.", "guide": "Configure safety filters, guardrails, risk detection, and content moderation."}, "eli5": "Check the banned-words list first, then read the danger rating on each label.", "learn": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/ai-services/content-safety/quickstart-blocklist"}, {"id": "m2-11", "type": "order", "skill": "D1 · Plan and manage", "stem": "A reporting app calls your Foundry resource with an API key stored in an app setting. Security requires that key-based access to the resource be switched off, and the app must keep working throughout the change. Which five actions should you perform in sequence?", "pool": ["Enable a managed identity on the app", "Assign the identity the Foundry User role on the project", "Change the app to use Microsoft Entra ID, then deploy and verify it", "Set disableLocalAuth to true on the Foundry resource", "Confirm that a request with the old key returns HTTP 401", "Store the key in Azure Key Vault", "Regenerate both keys on the resource"], "answer": [0, 1, 2, 3, 4], "think": {"premise": "That you switch keys off first and fix the app afterwards. Move the app to Entra ID and prove it works, then disable local auth, then confirm the key is refused, because the change can take time to reach the gateway.", "map": null, "apply": null, "rule": "Identity → role → keyless app verified → disableLocalAuth → confirm 401 with the old key.", "guide": "Configure security, including managed identity, private networking, keyless credentials, and role policies."}, "eli5": "Give everyone a badge and check the badge reader works before you change the locks."}, {"id": "m2-12", "type": "single", "skill": "D2 · GenAI and agents", "stem": "An agent uses an MCP tool on a partner's server that builds a quarterly inventory reconciliation. The server supports MCP tasks, and the tool takes about four minutes to finish. Every call fails after 100 seconds. The agent's model supports background mode. What should you do so the call can complete?", "options": ["Split the work into one user turn per step", "Raise the deployment's tokens-per-minute quota", "Move the reconciliation into the code interpreter", "Run the response in background mode and poll it"], "answer": 3, "runner": 2, "runnerWhy": "it can run long Python, but its sandbox has no outbound network, so it can't reach the partner's server.", "think": {"premise": "That a timeout is a capacity problem. Synchronous MCP calls stop at 100 seconds; background mode runs the response asynchronously.", "map": [["Tool call over 100 seconds", "Synchronous limit", "Background mode, poll the response"], ["Too many tokens per minute", "Quota", "HTTP 429, not a timeout"], ["Model-written code on files", "Sandbox", "Code interpreter (no network)"]], "apply": [["\"fails after 100 seconds\"", "The non-streaming MCP limit."], ["\"supports MCP tasks\" and \"supports background mode\"", "Both conditions for background mode are met."]], "rule": "Long MCP operations: background=True, then poll the response until it completes.", "guide": "Integrate agent tools, including APIs, knowledge stores, search, content understanding, and custom functions."}, "eli5": "Leave your number and get a call back instead of holding the line.", "learn": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/agents/how-to/tools/model-context-protocol"}, {"id": "m2-13", "type": "single", "skill": "D2 · GenAI and agents", "stem": "An agent answers warranty questions through the Azure AI Search tool with its default settings. Answers about multi-part warranty terms often miss conditions. Traces show the tool returns five short chunks, and the missing conditions sit in the sixth to tenth results. What should you change?", "options": ["Raise the model's reasoning effort to high", "Set query_type to vector_semantic_hybrid", "Increase top_k on the Azure AI Search tool", "Add file search over the warranty PDFs"], "answer": 2, "runner": 1, "runnerWhy": "it sounds like a relevance upgrade, but it's already the default, so nothing changes.", "think": {"premise": "That missing content means the query type is wrong. The right chunks are found; too few are passed to the model.", "map": [["Right chunks just past the cut-off", "Result count", "Raise top_k (default 5)"], ["Exact codes missed", "Query type", "Hybrid (already the default)"], ["Model reasons badly", "Model", "Reasoning effort"]], "apply": [["\"default settings\"", "top_k is 5 and query_type is already vector_semantic_hybrid."], ["\"sit in the sixth to tenth results\"", "Return more results."]], "rule": "Search tool defaults: query_type vector_semantic_hybrid, top_k 5.", "guide": "Integrate agent tools, including APIs, knowledge stores, search, content understanding, and custom functions."}, "eli5": "The answer is on the next page of results; ask for more pages.", "learn": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/agents/how-to/tools/ai-search"}, {"id": "m2-14", "type": "single", "skill": "D2 · GenAI and agents", "stem": "In one Foundry project, a triage agent talks to the customer and then hands billing questions to a billing agent. The billing agent must see the whole exchange so far, and the solution must not copy messages between agents or keep history in the application. What should you do?", "options": ["Give the triage agent an A2A tool for the billing agent", "Call the billing agent with the same conversation ID", "Export the exchange to a file search store for billing", "Create the billing agent in a project of its own"], "answer": 1, "runner": 0, "runnerWhy": "it lets one agent call another, but the called agent gets only what's passed in the call, not the shared conversation.", "think": {"premise": "That a conversation belongs to one agent. Agents in the same project share conversation storage.", "map": [["Same project, same customer chat", "Shared state", "Reuse the conversation ID"], ["Agent calls agent as a tool", "Delegation", "A2A tool"], ["Different project", "Isolation", "Can't see the conversation"]], "apply": [["\"In one Foundry project\"", "Conversation storage is shared."], ["\"must not copy messages\"", "Rules out passing history in a call or a file."]], "rule": "Conversations are project-scoped and agent-independent: hand over by conversation ID.", "guide": "Define agent roles, goals, conversation-tracking approach, and tool schemas."}, "eli5": "Pass the case file across the desk, not a summary on a sticky note.", "learn": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/agents/overview"}, {"id": "m2-15", "type": "single", "skill": "D2 · GenAI and agents", "stem": "A store-hours FAQ agent uses a reasoning model with reasoning_effort set to high. Answers are correct, but median latency is eight seconds and output token cost is well above forecast. Most questions are simple lookups. You need to cut latency and cost without risking truncated answers. What should you change?", "options": ["Lower max_output_tokens to 200", "Move the deployment to Provisioned", "Lower reasoning_effort to low", "Set temperature to 0"], "answer": 2, "runner": 0, "runnerWhy": "it caps cost, but the cap includes reasoning tokens, so answers can be cut off, which the stem forbids.", "think": {"premise": "That a token cap is the cost lever. On reasoning models, effort sets how much thinking happens; the cap only truncates.", "map": [["Too much thinking for easy questions", "Effort", "Lower reasoning_effort"], ["Hard cap on spend", "Cap", "max_output_tokens (includes reasoning)"], ["Randomness", "Sampling", "temperature (rejected by reasoning models)"]], "apply": [["\"Most questions are simple lookups\"", "High effort is wasted."], ["\"without risking truncated answers\"", "Rules out a tight max_output_tokens."]], "rule": "Reasoning models: tune reasoning_effort; max_output_tokens covers thinking plus answer.", "guide": "Tune generation behavior, such as prompt engineering and adjusting model parameters."}, "eli5": "Don't ask the professor to think hard about opening hours.", "learn": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/reasoning"}, {"id": "m2-16", "type": "single", "skill": "D2 · GenAI and agents", "stem": "A model classifies customer return reasons into a retailer's 14 reason codes. With zero-shot instructions, accuracy is 78%. You have 40 labelled examples, and the team must improve accuracy this week without starting a training project or changing the deployment. What should you do first?", "options": ["Fine-tune the model on the 40 labelled examples", "Raise the temperature so the model explores more codes", "Route requests through model router in Quality mode", "Add some labelled examples to the prompt as few-shot examples"], "answer": 3, "runner": 0, "runnerWhy": "it can raise accuracy, but it's a training project, and 40 examples is a thin dataset; prompt changes come first.", "think": {"premise": "That better accuracy needs training. Examples in the prompt often close the gap at no setup cost.", "map": [["Model misreads the label set", "Prompt", "Few-shot examples"], ["Prompting has plateaued, lots of data", "Training", "Fine-tuning"], ["Mixed difficulty, cost", "Routing", "Model router"]], "apply": [["\"without starting a training project\"", "Rules out fine-tuning."], ["\"or changing the deployment\"", "Rules out model router."], ["\"40 labelled examples\"", "Enough for a few-shot prompt."]], "rule": "Prompt engineering (instructions, few-shot) before fine-tuning.", "guide": "Tune generation behavior, such as prompt engineering and adjusting model parameters."}, "eli5": "Show the new clerk a few filled-in forms before sending them on a course.", "learn": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/concepts/prompt-engineering"}, {"id": "m2-17", "type": "multi", "pick": 2, "skill": "D2 · GenAI and agents", "stem": "Your company allows only generally available features in production agents. A design review lists the capabilities planned for a new customer-service agent in Foundry Agent Service. Which two capabilities must be removed from the design so that it meets the policy? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.", "options": ["Agent memory", "Code interpreter tool", "A2A tool", "Computer use tool", "Prompt agent definition"], "answer": [0, 3], "runner": 2, "runnerWhy": "it's newer than the others and sounds preview, but the A2A tool is GA.", "think": {"premise": "That newer means preview. The A2A tool is GA; memory and computer use are still preview.", "map": [["Remember users across chats", "Preview", "Memory"], ["Operate a desktop or browser", "Preview", "Computer use"], ["Call another agent", "GA", "A2A tool"], ["Instructions + tools", "GA", "Prompt agent"]], "apply": [["\"only generally available features\"", "Remove the preview items."]], "rule": "Preview: memory, computer use. GA: prompt agents, A2A tool, code interpreter.", "guide": "Choose appropriate memory, tool, and knowledge integration services for agent solutions."}, "eli5": "Two items on the menu are still being taste-tested.", "learn": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/agents/overview"}, {"id": "m2-18", "type": "yesno", "skill": "D2 · GenAI and agents", "stem": "You are choosing the orchestration approach for a set of agents that must stay in production for several years. For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "statements": [{"text": "Foundry Workflows are a good base for an orchestration that must run beyond December 2026.", "answer": false, "why": "Workflows are preview and retire on 1 December 2026."}, {"text": "Microsoft Agent Framework is the successor to Semantic Kernel and AutoGen.", "answer": true, "why": "Agent Framework 1.0 (Python and .NET) succeeds both."}, {"text": "Prompt flow is scheduled to retire in April 2027.", "answer": true, "why": "It retires on 20 April 2027."}, {"text": "Tracing is generally available for prompt agents and hosted agents.", "answer": true, "why": "It's preview only for workflow and external agents."}], "think": {"premise": "That anything in the Foundry portal is a safe long-term base. Workflows and prompt flow both have retirement dates.", "map": null, "apply": null, "rule": "Long-lived orchestration: Agent Framework. Workflows retire Dec 2026; prompt flow Apr 2027.", "guide": "Orchestrate multiple models, flows, or hybrid LLM and rules engines."}, "eli5": "Don't build your new house on a plot that's due to be cleared next year.", "learn": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/agents/overview"}, {"id": "m2-19", "type": "yesno", "skill": "D2 · GenAI and agents", "stem": "You are reviewing tool and data choices for agents in Foundry Agent Service. For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "statements": [{"text": "Agent memory can be isolated in your own virtual network.", "answer": false, "why": "Memory has no VNet support."}, {"text": "The Azure Functions tool requires the Standard agent setup.", "answer": true, "why": "It isn't available with the Basic setup."}, {"text": "Grounding with Bing keeps search queries inside the Azure compliance boundary.", "answer": false, "why": "Queries go outside the boundary."}, {"text": "The tool_choice parameter accepts auto, required and none.", "answer": true, "why": "Those are the three values."}], "think": {"premise": "That every agent feature inherits Azure's network and compliance controls. Memory has no VNet; Bing goes outside the boundary.", "map": null, "apply": null, "rule": "Memory: no VNet. Functions tool: Standard setup. Bing/web search: outside the boundary. tool_choice: auto/required/none.", "guide": "Integrate agent tools, including APIs, knowledge stores, search, content understanding, and custom functions."}, "eli5": "Some rooms in the building have no lock, and one door opens onto the street.", "learn": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/agents/overview"}, {"id": "m2-20", "type": "code", "skill": "D2 · GenAI and agents", "stem": "A returns agent calls a create_return function through the Responses API. Logs show that the model sometimes adds fields that aren't in the schema, and your code crashes on them. You need the arguments to always match the schema exactly. How should you complete the tool definition?", "code": "tools = [{\"type\": \"function\", \"name\": \"create_return\",\n  \"strict\": [[0]],\n  \"parameters\": {\"type\": \"object\",\n    \"properties\": {\"order_id\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"reason\": {\"type\": \"string\"}},\n    \"required\": [\"order_id\", \"reason\"],\n    \"[[1]]\": False}}]", "blanks": [{"options": ["True", "False", "\"auto\""], "answer": 0, "why": "strict mode makes the arguments follow the schema."}, {"options": ["additionalProperties", "patternProperties", "unevaluatedItems"], "answer": 0, "why": "strict mode requires additionalProperties set to false, with every property listed in required."}], "think": {"premise": "That a clearer description stops invented fields. Strict mode enforces the schema.", "map": null, "apply": null, "rule": "Strict tool schema: strict true, every property required, additionalProperties false.", "guide": "Define agent roles, goals, conversation-tracking approach, and tool schemas."}, "eli5": "A form with fixed boxes instead of a blank page.", "learn": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/structured-outputs"}, {"id": "m2-21", "type": "order", "skill": "D2 · GenAI and agents", "stem": "A policy Q&A app must not return answers that its retrieved passages don't support. You plan a self-check in which a second generation call happens only when the check fails, so most requests need one generation call. Which four steps should the request pipeline run, in order?", "pool": ["Generate a draft answer from the retrieved passages", "Score the draft's groundedness against the passages", "If the score is below the threshold, send the draft and critique back for revision", "Return the final answer and log the score", "Raise reasoning effort to max on every request", "Return the draft to the user before scoring it"], "answer": [0, 1, 2, 3], "think": {"premise": "That self-critique means always calling the model twice. Check first, revise only on failure.", "map": null, "apply": null, "rule": "Draft → check → revise only if it fails → return and log.", "guide": "Implement model reflection, chain-of-thought evaluations, and self-critique loops."}, "eli5": "Proofread the letter, and rewrite it only if the proofreader finds a mistake."}, {"id": "m2-25", "type": "single", "skill": "D3 · Computer vision", "stem": "A retail chain wants a store app that recognises enrolled loyalty members by face at the entrance and greets them by name. Members will opt in. The team has a new Azure subscription with no special approvals. What must happen before the solution can identify members by face?", "options": ["The team must train a Custom Vision model on member photos", "Microsoft must approve a Face limited-access application", "The team must send member photos to a gpt-5 vision model", "Members must upload photos that carry C2PA Content Credentials"], "answer": 1, "runner": 2, "runnerWhy": "a vision model can describe a face, but it isn't a face identification service, and it doesn't bypass the access controls on identifying people.", "think": {"premise": "That opt-in consent is enough to start. Face identification and verification need a limited-access approval first.", "map": [["Identify or verify a person", "Limited access", "Face API approval"], ["Describe an image", "General", "Vision model"], ["Custom image classes", "Retiring", "Custom Vision"], ["Provenance of generated images", "Metadata", "C2PA"]], "apply": [["\"recognises enrolled ... members by face\"", "Identification."], ["\"no special approvals\"", "Apply first."]], "rule": "Face identification and verification: limited access.", "guide": "Implement solutions that identify objects, components, or regions within images or video."}, "eli5": "Some doors need a pass from head office, even if the visitor is happy to come in.", "learn": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/ai-services/computer-vision/overview-identity"}, {"id": "m2-26", "type": "single", "skill": "D3 · Computer vision", "stem": "An online furniture store has separate studio photos of a sofa, a floor lamp and a rug. Marketing wants one realistic room scene that shows these exact products together, using only generally available models and no photographer. Your subscription has no limited-access approvals. What should you do?", "options": ["Generate a sora-2 video of the room and save a still frame from it", "Call images.edit on gpt-image-2 with the three photos as input images", "Call images.edit on gpt-image-1 with the three photos as input images", "Call images.generate on gpt-image-2 with a description of each product"], "answer": 1, "runner": 3, "runnerWhy": "it produces a room scene, but the products are invented from the description, not the real ones in the photos.", "think": {"premise": "That editing means changing one picture. An edit can take several input images and compose them.", "map": [["Use real product photos", "Edit with inputs", "images.edit (up to 16 input images)"], ["Invent from text", "Generate", "images.generate"], ["Older model", "Limited access", "gpt-image-1"], ["Video", "Preview", "sora-2"]], "apply": [["\"these exact products\"", "The photos must be inputs."], ["\"only generally available models\" and \"no limited-access approvals\"", "gpt-image-2; rules out gpt-image-1 and sora-2."]], "rule": "Combine real images → images.edit with several input images on a GA model.", "guide": "Generate and edit images by using generative image models."}, "eli5": "Give the artist the actual furniture to paint, not a description of it.", "learn": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/dall-e"}, {"id": "m2-27", "type": "yesno", "skill": "D3 · Computer vision", "stem": "You are reviewing the design of an app that edits product photos and publishes AI-generated images. For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "statements": [{"text": "Content Credentials can be lost when an image is re-encoded or screenshotted.", "answer": true, "why": "It's metadata, which can be stripped."}, {"text": "An image without Content Credentials can be treated as proof that it wasn't AI-generated.", "answer": false, "why": "It proves origin when present, not absence when missing."}, {"text": "A transparent background requires PNG output.", "answer": true, "why": "background=transparent needs PNG."}, {"text": "In an edit mask, opaque pixels mark the area that the model should change.", "answer": false, "why": "Transparent pixels are edited; opaque pixels are kept."}], "think": {"premise": "That provenance works both ways, and that a mask paints the edit area. Content Credentials prove presence only; transparency marks the edit.", "map": null, "apply": null, "rule": "C2PA: metadata, strippable, proof only when present. Transparent background: PNG. Mask: transparent = edit.", "guide": "Generate and edit images by using generative image models."}, "eli5": "A signature proves who painted it; a missing signature proves nothing. The hole in the stencil is where the paint goes.", "learn": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/ai-foundry/openai/concepts/content-credentials"}, {"id": "m2-28", "type": "code", "skill": "D3 · Computer vision", "stem": "A warehouse app sends photos of shipping labels to a gpt-5-mini deployment through the Responses API and asks for the tracking number. The numbers are printed in very small type, and accuracy matters more than token cost. How should you complete the code?", "code": "r = client.responses.create(model=\"gpt-5-mini\", input=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": [\n    {\"type\": \"input_text\", \"text\": \"Return the tracking number only.\"},\n    {\"type\": \"[[0]]\", \"image_url\": data_url, \"detail\": \"[[1]]\"}]}])", "blanks": [{"options": ["input_image", "image_url", "input_file"], "answer": 0, "why": "the Responses API content type for an image; image_url is the field inside it."}, {"options": ["high", "low", "auto"], "answer": 0, "why": "high reads fine print at a higher token cost; low saves tokens and loses detail."}], "think": {"premise": "That the model sees every image at full detail. The detail setting trades tokens for fine print.", "map": null, "apply": null, "rule": "Responses API image: {type: input_image, image_url, detail}. Fine print → high; cost first → low.", "guide": "Analyze images and video by using multimodal models and Content Understanding."}, "eli5": "Hand over the magnifying glass when the print is tiny.", "learn": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/gpt-with-vision"}, {"id": "m2-29", "type": "single", "skill": "D4 · Text analysis", "stem": "Your code extracts order details from support emails by using structured outputs in strict mode. The gift_message field appears in only some emails, and requests fail schema validation when you remove it from the required list. You need the field to be optional while strict mode stays on. What should you do?", "options": ["Turn off strict mode and switch to JSON mode", "Remove it from required and allow additional properties", "Keep it in required and make its type string or null", "Ask for gift_message in a second request when present"], "answer": 2, "runner": 0, "runnerWhy": "JSON mode returns valid JSON, but not necessarily your schema, and the code relies on the schema.", "think": {"premise": "That optional means leaving a field out of required. In strict mode every property is required, so optional means nullable.", "map": [["Field sometimes absent, strict mode", "Schema", "Nullable type, still required"], ["Valid JSON, any shape", "JSON mode", "No schema guarantee"], ["Extra keys", "additionalProperties", "Must be false in strict mode"]], "apply": [["\"requests fail ... when you remove it from the required list\"", "Strict mode needs every property in required."], ["\"while strict mode stays on\"", "Rules out JSON mode."]], "rule": "Strict mode: all properties required, additionalProperties false; optional = [\"string\", \"null\"].", "guide": "Implement solutions to extract entities, topics, summaries, and structured JSON outputs by using generative prompting and Foundry Tools."}, "eli5": "Every box on the form stays; you can write 'none' in it.", "learn": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/structured-outputs"}, {"id": "m2-30", "type": "single", "skill": "D4 · Text analysis", "stem": "A pharmacy chain must pull medication names, dosages and frequencies from free-text clinical notes, and keep the link between each medication and its dosage. No one on the team can label training data, and the output must use medical entity types. What should you use?", "options": ["Key phrase extraction in Azure Language", "PII detection with the PHI domain enabled", "Text Analytics for health in Azure Language", "Custom named entity recognition in Azure Language"], "answer": 2, "runner": 3, "runnerWhy": "it can learn medication entities, but only from labelled examples, which nobody can provide, and it doesn't return relations.", "think": {"premise": "That domain entities always need a custom model. Text Analytics for health is prebuilt for medical entities and their relations.", "map": [["Medical entities and relations, no labelling", "Prebuilt", "Text Analytics for health"], ["Your own entity types", "Trained on labels", "Custom NER"], ["Hide protected health information", "Redaction", "PII with PHI"], ["Main topics", "Legacy", "Key phrases"]], "apply": [["\"keep the link between each medication and its dosage\"", "Relations."], ["\"No one ... can label training data\"", "Rules out custom NER."]], "rule": "Clinical text: Text Analytics for health extracts medical entities and relations.", "guide": "Implement text analysis, such as entity extraction, sentiment analysis, and PII detection."}, "eli5": "Use the doctor's reading glasses, not a general dictionary.", "learn": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/ai-services/language-service/text-analytics-for-health/overview"}, {"id": "m2-31", "type": "yesno", "skill": "D4 · Text analysis", "stem": "You are planning text and speech features for a pharmacy chain's support channels. For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "statements": [{"text": "Custom named entity recognition is trained on labelled examples of your entities.", "answer": true, "why": "You label examples; it learns them."}, {"text": "The gpt-realtime model is in preview.", "answer": false, "why": "It's GA."}, {"text": "The Voice Live API includes noise suppression and echo cancellation.", "answer": true, "why": "Both are built in, with semantic turn detection."}, {"text": "A batch document translation job returns the translated files in the response to the request that starts it.", "answer": false, "why": "It's asynchronous; you poll the job and read the target container."}], "think": {"premise": "That every service answers in the same call and every voice model is preview. Batch is asynchronous; gpt-realtime is GA.", "map": null, "apply": null, "rule": "Custom NER: labelled. gpt-realtime: GA. Voice Live: noise suppression, echo cancellation. Batch translation: poll.", "guide": "Implement speech-to-text and text-to-speech."}, "eli5": "Some jobs you drop off and collect later; some come back while you wait.", "learn": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/ai-services/speech-service/voice-live"}, {"id": "m2-32", "type": "code", "skill": "D4 · Text analysis", "stem": "A store kiosk must listen to a customer speaking English and show live captions in French and Spanish at the same time, by using a single recognizer for both languages. Authentication is already configured. You need to complete the Speech SDK code. How should you complete the code?", "code": "cfg = make_translation_config()   # Entra ID auth, set up elsewhere\ncfg.speech_recognition_language = \"en-US\"\nfor lang in (\"fr\", \"es\"):\n    cfg.[[0]](lang)\nrec = speechsdk.translation.[[1]](translation_config=cfg, audio_config=mic)", "blanks": [{"options": ["add_target_language", "set_target_language", "target_languages"], "answer": 0, "why": "adds each output language to the translation config."}, {"options": ["TranslationRecognizer", "SpeechRecognizer", "IntentRecognizer"], "answer": 0, "why": "returns translations; SpeechRecognizer returns only same-language text."}], "think": {"premise": "That speech translation needs a separate call per language. One TranslationRecognizer can return several target languages.", "map": null, "apply": null, "rule": "SpeechTranslationConfig + add_target_language per language → TranslationRecognizer.", "guide": "Translate text and documents by using Azure Translator."}, "eli5": "One interpreter who whispers into two ears at once.", "learn": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/ai-services/speech-service/get-started-speech-translation"}, {"id": "m2-33", "type": "order", "skill": "D4 · Text analysis", "stem": "Each evening, about 2,000 recorded store calls are uploaded to a Blob Storage container. Transcripts must be ready by the next morning at the lowest cost, and the Speech resource must read the audio without storage keys. Which four actions should you perform in sequence?", "pool": ["Grant the Speech resource's managed identity read access to the container", "Create a batch transcription job that points to the container", "Poll the job until its status is Succeeded", "Download the transcription result files", "Call recognize_once for each recording", "Train a custom neural voice on the recordings"], "answer": [0, 1, 2, 3], "think": {"premise": "That you transcribe stored files one call at a time. Batch transcription reads a container asynchronously.", "map": null, "apply": null, "rule": "Access → create batch job → poll → fetch results.", "guide": "Implement speech-to-text and text-to-speech."}, "eli5": "Give the typist a key to the tape room, drop off the order, check back, collect the transcripts."}, {"id": "m2-34", "type": "single", "skill": "D5 · Information extraction", "stem": "An index holds embeddings for 8,000 contract clauses. Legal reviewers require vector queries to always return the true nearest clauses, never an approximation, and a query latency of a second or two is acceptable. The index is small and rarely changes. Which vector search algorithm should you configure?", "options": ["Semantic ranker with a scoring profile", "HNSW with default parameters", "HNSW with a larger efSearch value", "Exhaustive KNN"], "answer": 3, "runner": 2, "runnerWhy": "a larger efSearch raises recall, but HNSW is still approximate, and the requirement is exact results.", "think": {"premise": "That HNSW can be tuned into exact search. Only exhaustive KNN guarantees the true nearest neighbours.", "map": [["Exact nearest neighbours, small index", "Exact", "Exhaustive KNN"], ["Fast at scale", "Approximate", "HNSW"], ["Better order of text results", "Ranking", "Semantic ranker"]], "apply": [["\"never an approximation\"", "Exhaustive KNN."], ["\"8,000\" and \"a second or two is acceptable\"", "Its cost doesn't matter here."]], "rule": "HNSW = approximate and fast; exhaustive KNN = exact and slower.", "guide": "Configure retrieval and indexing strategies, such as hybrid search, semantic search, and vector search."}, "eli5": "For a small library, check every shelf instead of guessing the likely aisle.", "learn": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/vector-search-overview"}, {"id": "m2-35", "type": "yesno", "skill": "D5 · Information extraction", "stem": "You are designing knowledge retrieval for agents that use Azure AI Search. For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "statements": [{"text": "Knowledge bases in Azure AI Search were previously called knowledge agents.", "answer": true, "why": "Renamed in November 2025."}, {"text": "Query planning in knowledge bases is generally available.", "answer": false, "why": "Only extractive retrieval is GA; query planning is preview."}, {"text": "Agents retrieve from a knowledge base by using the knowledge_base_retrieve tool.", "answer": true, "why": "That's the tool name."}, {"text": "Making an existing field filterable can usually be done without rebuilding the index.", "answer": false, "why": "Changing field attributes usually means a rebuild."}], "think": {"premise": "That every knowledge base feature is GA and index fields are easy to change. Only extractive retrieval is GA; attribute changes usually need a rebuild.", "map": null, "apply": null, "rule": "Knowledge bases (ex knowledge agents): extractive retrieval GA; planning and synthesis preview. Field attributes: plan them up front.", "guide": "Connect retrieval pipelines directly to workflows and agent tools."}, "eli5": "The new librarian has a new name; some of her skills are still on probation; and the shelves are hard to relabel.", "learn": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/agents/concepts/what-is-foundry-iq"}, {"id": "m2-36", "type": "code", "skill": "D5 · Information extraction", "stem": "A help-centre search page must show an AI-extracted caption under each result and, when the index contains one, a direct answer above the results. The index has a semantic configuration named default. You need to complete the Python query code. How should you complete the code?", "code": "results = search.search(search_text=q, query_type=\"semantic\",\n    semantic_configuration_name=\"default\", [[0]]=\"extractive\",\n    query_answer=\"extractive\", top=10)\nanswers = results.[[1]]()", "blanks": [{"options": ["query_caption", "caption_fields", "search_fields"], "answer": 0, "why": "asks the semantic ranker for captions; search_fields limits which fields are searched."}, {"options": ["get_answers", "get_captions", "get_facets"], "answer": 0, "why": "returns the semantic answers; captions come back on each result, facets are for filters."}], "think": {"premise": "That captions and answers come back automatically. You ask for them on a semantic query and read answers from the result set.", "map": null, "apply": null, "rule": "Semantic query: query_caption and query_answer = extractive; read results.get_answers().", "guide": "Configure retrieval and indexing strategies, such as hybrid search, semantic search, and vector search."}, "eli5": "Ask the librarian to underline the key line and, if she can, write the answer on the cover.", "learn": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/semantic-how-to-query-request"}, {"id": "m2-c1", "type": "single", "skill": "D4 · Text analysis", "stem": "You need to meet the speech requirement for order numbers and return codes, including the problem found in the phone pilot. What should the phone service do?", "options": ["Wrap each code in a prosody element with the rate set to slow", "Wrap each code in a phoneme element with an IPA pronunciation", "Wrap each code in a say-as element with interpret-as set to characters", "Add the codes to a phrase list on the speech recognizer"], "answer": 2, "runner": 1, "runnerWhy": "phoneme sets how a known word sounds, but every order number is different, so you'd have to write a pronunciation for each one.", "think": {"premise": "That pronunciation problems always need phonemes. Spelling a value out letter by letter is a content-type hint: say-as.", "map": [["Read a code letter by letter", "Content type", "say-as interpret-as=\"characters\""], ["A fixed word sounds wrong", "Pronunciation", "phoneme or a custom lexicon"], ["Too fast", "Speed", "prosody rate"], ["Recognizer mishears words", "Speech to text", "Phrase list"]], "apply": [["\"read out one character at a time\" (Requirements)", "say-as with characters (or spell-out)."], ["\"N W four thousand four hundred seventy-one B\" (Problem statements)", "The engine read digits as a number."], ["\"reads the agent's reply back ... by using SSML\" (Planned design)", "SSML is already in use."]], "rule": "say-as interpret-as=\"characters\" spells text out; phoneme fixes how a word sounds; prosody changes speed and pitch.", "guide": "Implement speech-to-text and text-to-speech."}, "eli5": "Tell the reader it's a code to spell, not a number to say.", "learn": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/ai-services/speech-service/speech-synthesis-markup-pronunciation", "case": "northwind"}, {"id": "m2-c2", "type": "yesno", "skill": "D4 · Text analysis", "stem": "You are designing the language and speech handling for Ask Northwind. For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "statements": [{"text": "Text translation in Azure Translator detects the source language automatically if you don't specify one.", "answer": true, "why": "Omit the source and it's detected."}, {"text": "Language detection in Azure Language returns a confidence score with the detected language.", "answer": true, "why": "Each result includes a confidence score."}, {"text": "If the phone service sends its SSML to speak_text_async, the say-as markup is applied.", "answer": false, "why": "speak_text_async reads the markup as text; use speak_ssml_async."}, {"text": "A phrase list must be trained as a custom speech model before it takes effect.", "answer": false, "why": "Phrase lists apply at runtime with no training."}], "think": {"premise": "That speech customisation always needs training, and that any speak call understands SSML. Phrase lists work at runtime; SSML needs the SSML method.", "map": null, "apply": null, "rule": "Translator auto-detects the source. Language detection gives a confidence. SSML → speak_ssml_async. Phrase lists: no training.", "guide": "Implement speech-to-text and text-to-speech."}, "eli5": "Hand the reader a script, not a letter with stage directions read aloud.", "learn": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/ai-services/speech-service/speech-synthesis-markup-pronunciation", "case": "northwind"}, {"id": "m2-c3", "type": "single", "skill": "D1 · Plan and manage", "stem": "You need to configure Ask Northwind's guardrail so that it meets the safety requirement. What should you do?", "options": ["Set the sexual and violence controls on output to the High blocking level", "Add a protected material for text control to the output intervention point", "Set the sexual and violence controls to annotate only, and filter in the app", "Set the sexual and violence controls on output to the Medium blocking level"], "answer": 0, "runner": 3, "runnerWhy": "Medium sounds like a sensible middle setting, but it blocks only medium- and high-severity content, so low-severity content gets through.", "think": {"premise": "That High means \"blocks only high-severity content\". The blocking level names how strict the control is: High is the strictest.", "map": [["Block low, medium and high severity", "Strictest", "High blocking level"], ["Block medium and high", "Default-like", "Medium"], ["Block only high", "Least strict", "Low"], ["Log, don't block", "Annotate", "Not available for agents"]], "apply": [["\"block ... at every severity level, including low severity\" (Requirements)", "Only High blocks low severity."], ["\"Ask Northwind\" is an agent", "Annotate-only isn't available for agents."]], "rule": "Blocking levels: Low blocks high only; Medium blocks medium and high; High blocks low, medium and high.", "guide": "Configure safety filters, guardrails, risk detection, and content moderation."}, "eli5": "Set the filter to its finest mesh so even small stones are caught.", "learn": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/guardrails/guardrails-overview", "case": "northwind"}, {"id": "m2-c4", "type": "yesno", "skill": "D1 · Plan and manage", "stem": "You are planning access for Ask Northwind. For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement meets the requirements and is true. Otherwise, select No. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "statements": [{"text": "Because the project was created with the Azure CLI, Foundry User wasn't assigned to the project's managed identity automatically.", "answer": true, "why": "Only portal creation auto-assigns it; CLI and SDK deployments don't."}, {"text": "Assigning Foundry User to the phone service's identity, as the developer suggested, meets the access requirement.", "answer": false, "why": "Foundry User can create and change agents; the phone service may only call Ask Northwind, so use Foundry Agent Consumer scoped to that agent."}, {"text": "Foundry Project Manager is the minimum built-in role that lets Ana publish the agent to Microsoft Teams.", "answer": true, "why": "It's the least role that can publish agents."}, {"text": "Assigning Cognitive Services Usages Reader to the capacity-planning team at the resource group scope lets them view model quota.", "answer": false, "why": "It must be assigned at subscription scope."}], "think": {"premise": "That a broader role is harmless if it's convenient. Least privilege means the smallest role at the smallest scope, and some roles only work at one scope.", "map": null, "apply": null, "rule": "CLI-created projects need role assignments by hand. Call only: Agent Consumer. Publish: Project Manager. Quota: Usages Reader at subscription.", "guide": "Configure security, including managed identity, private networking, keyless credentials, and role policies."}, "eli5": "The phone line gets a caller's pass, not a builder's badge, and the fuel-gauge pass covers the whole depot.", "learn": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/concepts/rbac-foundry", "case": "northwind"}, {"id": "m2-c5", "type": "single", "skill": "D2 · GenAI and agents", "stem": "You need to connect Ask Northwind to the Orders API so that it meets the identity and orders requirements. What should you do?", "options": ["Add an OpenAPI tool that uses a project connection with managed identity authentication", "Add an Azure Functions tool that wraps each Orders API operation in a function", "Add a function tool, and have the chat widget and phone service call the Orders API", "Add an OpenAPI tool that uses a project connection with API key authentication"], "answer": 0, "runner": 2, "runnerWhy": "function calling works, but the app then runs every order call in its own code, which the requirements forbid.", "think": {"premise": "That an agent can only reach your API through your app. The OpenAPI tool calls the API itself, using the project's managed identity.", "map": [["API with an OpenAPI spec, Entra only", "Agent calls it", "OpenAPI tool, managed identity connection"], ["App executes the call", "Function calling", "Your code runs it"], ["API keys", "Key auth", "Disabled and forbidden"]], "apply": [["\"publishes an OpenAPI 3.0 description and accepts only Microsoft Entra ID tokens\" (Existing environment)", "OpenAPI tool with managed identity."], ["\"must not run order lookups or returns in their own code\" (Requirements)", "Rules out function tools."], ["\"No API keys\"", "Rules out key auth."]], "rule": "OpenAPI tool auth: anonymous, API key, or project managed identity (connection with an audience).", "guide": "Integrate agent tools, including APIs, knowledge stores, search, content understanding, and custom functions."}, "eli5": "Give the assistant a staff badge for the stockroom instead of fetching every item yourself.", "learn": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/agents/how-to/tools/openapi", "case": "northwind"}, {"id": "m2-c6", "type": "single", "skill": "D2 · GenAI and agents", "stem": "You need to recommend how the operations team should run the weekly quality scoring. What should you recommend?", "options": ["Run the Customer Satisfaction evaluator at the conversation level", "Run the Intent Resolution evaluator at the conversation level", "Run the Customer Satisfaction evaluator at the turn level", "Run the Coherence and Fluency evaluators on every reply"], "answer": 0, "runner": 2, "runnerWhy": "right evaluator, wrong level: turn level scores single replies, and the requirement asks for whole conversations.", "think": {"premise": "That every evaluator can score a whole conversation. Only a few support conversation level, and Customer Satisfaction is one.", "map": [["Customer happy by the end?", "Conversation level", "Customer Satisfaction"], ["Understood each request", "Turn level only", "Intent Resolution"], ["Well-written replies", "Turn level", "Coherence, Fluency"]], "apply": [["\"score complete conversations, not single replies\" (Requirements)", "Conversation level, with data in the messages format."], ["\"whether the customer seemed satisfied\"", "Customer Satisfaction."]], "rule": "Conversation level: Task Completion, Customer Satisfaction and the two bundles; the rest are turn level.", "guide": "Integrate monitoring into deployed agents, evaluate agent behavior, and perform error analysis."}, "eli5": "Ask how the whole visit went, not whether each sentence was polite.", "learn": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/concepts/built-in-evaluators", "case": "northwind"}, {"id": "m2-c7", "type": "single", "skill": "D2 · GenAI and agents", "stem": "You need to resolve the language problem found in the pilot while meeting the language requirement. What should you do?", "options": ["Translate the policies index into French and Spanish with document translation", "Instruct the agent to reply in the language of the customer's latest message", "Call Translator on each message before and after the agent processes it", "Create one agent per language and route customers by their browser language"], "answer": 1, "runner": 2, "runnerWhy": "it would work, but it adds a service and two extra calls to every chat turn, and the model already writes French and Spanish.", "think": {"premise": "That retrieved English passages force an English answer. The model can read English sources and answer in another language when told to.", "map": [["Answer language drifts to the sources", "Instructions", "State the reply language"], ["Model can't handle the language", "Pipeline", "Translator before and after"], ["Content must exist in each language", "Copies", "Document translation"]], "apply": [["\"won't maintain translated copies\" (Requirements)", "Rules out translating the index."], ["\"as few extra services as possible\"", "Rules out a Translator chain."], ["\"apparently because every retrieved policy passage was in English\" (Problem statements)", "An instruction problem."]], "rule": "Use instructions first; add services only when the model can't do the job.", "guide": "Tune generation behavior, such as prompt engineering and adjusting model parameters."}, "eli5": "Tell the bilingual clerk to answer in the customer's language, even if the manual is in English.", "learn": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/concepts/prompt-engineering", "case": "northwind"}, {"id": "m2-c8", "type": "single", "skill": "D5 · Information extraction", "stem": "You are defining the Content Understanding analyzer for material test reports. You need to meet the requirement for test reports. How should you define the heat-treatment field?", "options": ["As a field that uses the classify method", "As a field that uses the extract method", "Use prebuilt-layout and summarise its text in code", "As a field that uses the generate method"], "answer": 3, "runner": 1, "runnerWhy": "extract copies a value that appears in the document, but a two-sentence summary across several pages doesn't appear anywhere.", "think": {"premise": "That every field is copied from the page. Content Understanding can extract, generate or classify a field value.", "map": [["Value printed in the document", "Verbatim", "extract"], ["Value composed from the content", "Synthesised", "generate"], ["One of a fixed set of labels", "Category", "classify"]], "apply": [["\"two-sentence summary of each report's heat-treatment section\" (Requirements)", "Not on the page: generate."], ["\"often runs across several pages\" (Documents)", "Rules out copying one passage."]], "rule": "Field methods: extract (verbatim), generate (summaries, derived values), classify (fixed categories).", "guide": "Extract structured content from documents, forms, and images by using Content Understanding."}, "eli5": "Ask the reader to write a short summary, not to photocopy a line.", "learn": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/ai-services/content-understanding/overview", "case": "litware"}, {"id": "m2-c9", "type": "single", "skill": "D5 · Information extraction", "stem": "You need to resolve the indexer problem in the pilot while meeting the search and security requirements. What should you do?", "options": ["Use a data source connection string that contains the storage key", "Allow the search service's outbound IP address in the litdocs firewall", "Create a private endpoint for the search service in the plant network", "Create a shared private link from the search service to litdocs"], "answer": 3, "runner": 2, "runnerWhy": "a search private endpoint controls traffic coming into the search service; the indexer's outbound calls to litdocs still have no private route.", "think": {"premise": "That one private endpoint covers both directions. Inbound traffic to search uses a private endpoint; the indexer's outbound traffic to a private data source uses a shared private link.", "map": [["Indexer must reach private storage", "Outbound", "Shared private link (approve it on the storage account)"], ["Clients must reach search privately", "Inbound", "Search private endpoint"], ["Storage public access disabled", "Firewall", "IP rules no longer apply"]], "apply": [["\"public network access ... to Disabled\" (Existing environment)", "Rules out IP allow-listing."], ["\"without public network access being turned back on\" (Requirements)", "A private route is needed."], ["\"No API keys ... for Storage\"", "Rules out a key in the connection string."]], "rule": "Indexer to private data source: shared private link + the search service's managed identity.", "guide": "Implement content extraction and indexing pipelines by using Azure AI Search."}, "eli5": "Build the search service its own private road out to the warehouse.", "learn": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/search-indexer-howto-access-private", "case": "litware"}, {"id": "m2-c10", "type": "yesno", "skill": "D1 · Plan and manage", "stem": "You are planning security for Litware's search solution. For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement meets the requirements and is true. Otherwise, select No. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "statements": [{"text": "Because role-based access is enabled on the search service, you can disable API keys on it.", "answer": true, "why": "With RBAC on, you can switch keys off."}, {"text": "Assigning Search Service Contributor to the web app's identity lets it run queries against the index.", "answer": false, "why": "It manages objects but can't query data; use Search Index Data Reader."}, {"text": "The indexer can read litdocs by using the search service's managed identity with Storage Blob Data Reader.", "answer": true, "why": "Indexers reach data sources with the search service's identity."}, {"text": "Storing the search admin key in Azure Key Vault and reading it from the web app meets the requirements.", "answer": false, "why": "It's still an API key, and an admin key is far more than query access."}], "think": {"premise": "That service-level roles include data access, and that a vault makes keys acceptable. Contributor roles manage; data roles query; keys are keys wherever they live.", "map": null, "apply": null, "rule": "RBAC on → keys can go. Query only → Search Index Data Reader. Indexer → search service identity + Storage Blob Data Reader.", "guide": "Configure security, including managed identity, private networking, keyless credentials, and role policies."}, "eli5": "The building manager can rearrange the shelves but isn't allowed to read the files.", "learn": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/search-security-rbac", "case": "litware"}, {"id": "m2-c11", "type": "single", "skill": "D5 · Information extraction", "stem": "Packing-slip fields must flow into Litware's ERP system. You need to choose the extraction approach that meets the packing-slip and cost requirements. Which approach should you choose?", "options": ["A Document Intelligence custom template model", "Content Understanding prebuilt-read with parsing code", "A Document Intelligence custom neural model", "A Content Understanding custom analyzer"], "answer": 0, "runner": 3, "runnerWhy": "it would extract the fields, but its generative fields run on your LLM deployment, which the packing-slip requirement forbids.", "think": {"premise": "That the newest service always wins. A fixed layout with labelled samples and no LLM allowed is the custom template case.", "map": [["One fixed layout, labelled samples", "Trained, layout-bound", "DI custom template"], ["Varied structured layouts, labelled samples", "Trained, flexible", "DI custom neural"], ["Your schema, no labelling", "Generative", "Content Understanding analyzer (uses your LLM)"], ["Text only", "No fields", "prebuilt-read / layout"]], "apply": [["\"one fixed layout that hasn't changed in five years\" (Documents)", "Custom template."], ["\"labelled 60 sample packing slips\"", "Training data exists."], ["\"must not depend on any large language model deployment\" (Requirements)", "Rules out a Content Understanding analyzer."]], "rule": "Fixed layout → custom template; varied layouts → custom neural; no labels → Content Understanding analyzer.", "guide": "Extract structured content from documents, forms, and images by using Content Understanding."}, "eli5": "A stencil works perfectly when every form is printed the same way.", "learn": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/ai-services/document-intelligence/train/custom-model", "case": "litware"}, {"id": "m2-c12", "type": "single", "skill": "D3 · Computer vision", "stem": "You need to recommend a solution for the nameplate photos that meets the requirements and resolves the problem found in the prototype. What should you recommend?", "options": ["gpt-5-mini with structured outputs and image detail set to high", "Image Analysis 4.0 OCR on each photo, with parsing code for the values", "A Custom Vision object detection model trained on nameplate photos", "A Content Understanding analyzer with a field schema for the four values"], "answer": 3, "runner": 0, "runnerWhy": "it fixes the varying answer shape and reads small print better, but it doesn't return a confidence score or the location of each value.", "think": {"premise": "That a vision model with a schema is enough for extraction at scale. Confidence scores and the location of each value point to Content Understanding.", "map": [["Fields + confidence + where on the image", "Repeatable extraction", "Content Understanding analyzer"], ["Ask a question about an image", "Answers", "Vision model"], ["New captioning or OCR build", "Deprecated", "Image Analysis 4.0"], ["Train a detector", "Retiring", "Custom Vision"]], "apply": [["\"confidence score and the location on the image\" (Requirements)", "Content Understanding returns both."], ["\"shape of its answers varied\" (Problem statements)", "A field schema fixes it."], ["\"about 2,000 photos a day\"", "Repeatable extraction at scale."]], "rule": "For bounding regions, confidence and repeatable extraction at scale, prefer Content Understanding over a chat-style vision call.", "guide": "Analyze images and video by using multimodal models and Content Understanding."}, "eli5": "Use a form with labelled boxes and a 'how sure are you' column, not a free-text note.", "learn": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/ai-services/content-understanding/overview", "case": "litware"}, {"id": "m2-c13", "type": "yesno", "skill": "D3 · Computer vision", "stem": "You are evaluating services for Litware's images and inspection videos. For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "statements": [{"text": "Content Understanding can return a transcript and a description of each segment of an inspection video.", "answer": true, "why": "Video output includes a transcript, key frames and segment descriptions."}, {"text": "Content Understanding can split a video into segments by categories that Litware defines.", "answer": true, "why": "You can segment by your own categories."}, {"text": "Image Analysis 4.0 is the recommended service for new captioning of the nameplate photos.", "answer": false, "why": "It's deprecated for new builds."}, {"text": "A vision model returns the same output structure for every photo even without structured outputs.", "answer": false, "why": "Without a schema, the answer shape can vary."}], "think": {"premise": "That every vision service is current and every model answer is stable. Image Analysis 4.0 is deprecated; free-form answers vary.", "map": null, "apply": null, "rule": "Video: Content Understanding (transcript, key frames, segments, your categories). Stable shape: schema or analyzer.", "guide": "Analyze images and video by using multimodal models and Content Understanding."}, "eli5": "Chapters you name yourself, and a form instead of a free-text note.", "learn": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/ai-services/content-understanding/overview", "case": "litware"}, {"id": "m2-c14", "type": "order", "skill": "D5 · Information extraction", "stem": "You are building the review step for certificates of conformity. Which four actions should the pipeline perform for each certificate, in sequence?", "pool": ["Submit the certificate to the analyzer's analyze operation", "Poll the operation until it reports success", "Compare each field's confidence with 0.85", "Send low-confidence fields to an engineer and the rest to the ERP system", "Label 50 certificates and train a custom neural model", "Rerank the extracted fields with the semantic ranker"], "answer": [0, 1, 2, 3], "think": {"premise": "That extraction returns at once and is either right or wrong. Analyze is long-running, and confidence decides who checks each field.", "map": null, "apply": null, "rule": "Analyze → poll → compare confidence → route.", "guide": "Extract structured content from documents, forms, and images by using Content Understanding."}, "eli5": "Send it off, wait for the reply, check how sure it is, then decide who looks at it.", "learn": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/ai-services/content-understanding/overview", "case": "litware"}, {"id": "m2-s1", "type": "solution", "skill": "D2 · GenAI and agents", "scenario": "A support agent in Foundry Agent Service must keep each customer's conversation history across a chat that can span several days and devices. **Goal:** all conversation data must be stored at rest in Azure resources that the company owns, and only generally available features may be used.", "stem": "**Proposed solution:** You create the project with the Standard agent setup, connecting your own Azure Cosmos DB, Storage and AI Search resources, and the app reuses one conversation per customer chat by its ID. Does this meet the goal?", "answer": true, "think": {"premise": "That keeping history needs a special feature. Conversations already persist; the setup decides where they're stored.", "map": [["Agent state in your own resources", "Setup", "Standard setup (your Cosmos DB, Storage, AI Search)"], ["Microsoft-managed storage", "Setup", "Basic setup"], ["Facts about a user across chats", "Preview", "Memory"]], "apply": [["\"Standard agent setup ... your own Azure Cosmos DB\"", "Conversations stored in your resources: met."], ["\"reuses one conversation ... by its ID\"", "GA conversation state across days and devices: met."]], "rule": "Standard setup stores conversations and responses in your own Cosmos DB.", "guide": "Define agent roles, goals, conversation-tracking approach, and tool schemas."}, "eli5": "Keep the case files in your own filing cabinet, not the landlord's, and don't rely on a filing system still on trial.", "learn": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/agents/concepts/standard-agent-setup"}, {"id": "m2-s2", "type": "solution", "skill": "D2 · GenAI and agents", "scenario": "A support agent in Foundry Agent Service must keep each customer's conversation history across a chat that can span several days and devices. **Goal:** all conversation data must be stored at rest in Azure resources that the company owns, and only generally available features may be used.", "stem": "**Proposed solution:** You create the project with the Basic agent setup, and the app reuses one conversation per customer chat by its ID. Does this meet the goal?", "answer": false, "think": {"premise": "That all setups store data the same way. Basic setup uses Microsoft-managed resources.", "map": [["Agent state in your own resources", "Setup", "Standard setup (your Cosmos DB, Storage, AI Search)"], ["Microsoft-managed storage", "Setup", "Basic setup"], ["Facts about a user across chats", "Preview", "Memory"]], "apply": [["\"Basic agent setup\"", "Data at rest in Microsoft-managed storage: not met."], ["\"reuses one conversation\"", "Continuity is fine, but one failure is enough."]], "rule": "Basic = Microsoft-managed storage; Standard = bring your own.", "guide": "Define agent roles, goals, conversation-tracking approach, and tool schemas."}, "eli5": "Keep the case files in your own filing cabinet, not the landlord's, and don't rely on a filing system still on trial.", "learn": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/agents/concepts/standard-agent-setup"}, {"id": "m2-s3", "type": "solution", "skill": "D2 · GenAI and agents", "scenario": "A support agent in Foundry Agent Service must keep each customer's conversation history across a chat that can span several days and devices. **Goal:** all conversation data must be stored at rest in Azure resources that the company owns, and only generally available features may be used.", "stem": "**Proposed solution:** You create the project with the Standard agent setup, and the agent uses memory scoped to each customer, instead of conversations, to hold the chat history. Does this meet the goal?", "answer": false, "think": {"premise": "That memory is the way to keep a chat going. Memory is preview, and it keeps extracted facts, not the full history.", "map": [["Agent state in your own resources", "Setup", "Standard setup (your Cosmos DB, Storage, AI Search)"], ["Microsoft-managed storage", "Setup", "Basic setup"], ["Facts about a user across chats", "Preview", "Memory"]], "apply": [["\"uses memory\"", "Preview: not met."], ["\"Standard agent setup\"", "Storage is fine, but one failure is enough."]], "rule": "Within a chat, use conversations; memory (preview) is for facts across chats.", "guide": "Define agent roles, goals, conversation-tracking approach, and tool schemas."}, "eli5": "Keep the case files in your own filing cabinet, not the landlord's, and don't rely on a filing system still on trial.", "learn": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/agents/concepts/standard-agent-setup"}]};